<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kara Del</h1><p class="st-meta">1389 CE – 1513 CE (approx.) · capital Hami</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Mongol-ruled oasis kingdom at Hami, gateway between Ming China and Central Asia, which fell under the control of Turfan in the early 16th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghisid-principle">Chinggisid</a> family descended from Chagatai held the oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Hami</a> (Kumul) at the eastern end of the Tarim routes. Its prince Gunashiri sent tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> from the 1380s, and in 1404 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> recognised his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engke-temur">Engke Temür</a> as Prince of Zhongshun and soon set up the Hami Guard.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kara Del controlled the road between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a>, and its rulers lived by taxing caravans and by the tribute trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its population included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and Hui; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was giving way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> treated it as the key outpost of its western frontier system.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 1470s the Muslim rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a>, descendants of the khans of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, repeatedly seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Hami</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming</a> attempts to restore its princes failed. In 1513 Hami submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansur-khan">Mansur Khan</a> of Turfan, and the kingdom ended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> became the dominant faith of the oasis.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kara-del.jpg" alt="Kara Del" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming_Turpan_conflict.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1389 CE – 1513 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hami</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gunashiri, Engke Temür</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Uyghur, Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hami Kingdom, Qara Del</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1451&amp;focus=kara-del" aria-label="Kara Del on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6940 656 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kara Del lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7260 912L7643 1242M7260 912L7154 908"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7643 1242h0M7154 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7260 912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7260 912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1451&amp;focus=kara-del">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

